  "query_preview": "..\nRest of today through Sunday: A ridge of high pressure will be\ndisplaced to east of region heading into the overnight period.\nMoisture ahead of an approaching cold front will spread high\nlevel clouds across the region. Otherwise, moisture will be\nlacking ahead of this front will little to no precipitation\nexpected; the Cascade crest will see light precipitation with \nit and that's about all. The front will bring significant \ncooling and tighten the westerly gradient with winds picking up \nthroughout the day on Saturday. Westerly winds sustained at \n10-20 mph with gusts up to 25-35 mph will be common across the \nexposed areas of the Columbia Basin. Peak wind speeds will be \nachieved in the afternoon before relaxing quickly as the sun \nsets in the evening. Light weight objects may be blown around if\nnot secured (e.g. Halloween decorations). Patchy blowing dust \nis also possible near recently worked fields, but much of the \ncrops planted this time of year have been done so already and \nthe expectation is that the risk of blowing dust is decreasing \ncompared to late September into early October. Winds may also \nresult in choppy lakes that pose a risk for those on small \ncrafts.\n\nSunday night through Wednesday night: Shortwave ridging of high\npressure then builds in behind the exiting cold front by Sunday.\nThere will be a considerable amount of drier air that pushes in\nbehind the cold front on Saturday. A clear night with dry air in\nplace will result in strong radiational cooling as winds weaken.\nLow temperatures Sunday morning figure to be on the chilly \nside. Temperatures in the northern mountain valleys are expected\nto cool down into the upper 20s, especially for the colder more\nsheltered areas. Much of the Columbia Basin is expected to see \ntemperatures down into the low to mid 30s. This will lead frost \nformation and anyone that protected plants from the freezing \ntemperatures earlier in the week should plan to cover sensitive \nvegetation Sunday night as well. Areas at the tail end of the \ngrowing season that may see frost Sunday morning includes the \nWenatchee Area and Moses Lake Area. The Lewiston-Clarkston \nValley is less likely to see temperatures cold enough for frost \nas dew points are expected to be above freezing with lows closer\nto around 40 degrees.\n\nThe weather then remains dry heading through the first part of\nnext week. An upper level trough deepens in the eastern Pacific\nwith a low closing off but it will dig far enough south to\nprimarily bring precipitation to California by Tuesday into\nWednesday. The Northwest will be under split flow with the\nnorthern branch of the jet stream directing moisture into\nBritish Columbia. Winds remain light and temperatures near\nnormal.\n\nThursday into Friday: The upper level low that pushes into\nCalifornia looks to eject northeast across the Great Basin and\nmay spread light precipitation primarily over the Idaho \nPanhandle by Thursday. We also may see light precipitation \nacross the Cascade crest at this time with snow levels dropping \ndown to around 5,000 feet Thursday night. There is the potential\nfor a light dusting of snow in the mountains in the Cascades. \nThe probability of any precipitation to occur over the Cascade \ncrest and in the Idaho Panhandle is between 30-40%. Not \nimpactful precipitation by any means and not expected to produce\nmuch wetting precipitation as well. The dry start to our fall \nlooks to continue and it isn't helping with the ongoing drought \nsituation that we are seeing across the region. /SVH",
